    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What your child is learning",
        "text": "To multiply by 7 and 9 using a 5s fact they already know. 7 = 5 + 2, so 7 × 6 = 5 × 6 + 2 × 6 = 30 + 12 = 42. 9 = 5 + 4, so 9 × 6 = 5 × 6 + 4 × 6 = 30 + 24 = 54. Either factor can be broken apart."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Try it with real things",
        "list": [
          "Make 7 rows of 6 coins. Slide the bottom 2 rows away: 30 + 12 = 42.",
          "Count by 5s on fingers, then add the small fact."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Questions to ask",
        "list": [
          "What 5s fact can you start with?",
          "What is left after the 5? Is it a 2s fact or a 4s fact?",
          "Does your answer match the array?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Common mistakes",
        "list": [
          "Breaking 9 into 5 + 5 (that makes 10).",
          "Adding the leftover number instead of its fact: 30 + 2 instead of 30 + 12.",
          "Stopping at the 5s fact.",
          "Adding the factors: 7 + 6 = 13."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "A note on 9s",
        "text": "Some children also learn 9 × 6 as 10 × 6 minus one group of 6. Study Scoop sticks to the unit's add-on strategy (5s fact plus 4s fact), which uses facts your child already knows."
      }
    ]
  }
